= 3. Wars and Peace with Pakistan: Kashmir, Tashkent and Shimla

India's relationship with Pakistan has been defined by chronic geopolitical rivalry, ideological divergence, and recurring armed conflicts:

== The 1947–1948 Conflict & The Indus Waters Treaty (1960)
- *The 1947 Kashmir War:* Immediately after partition, Pakistani tribal invaders backed by the regular army invaded Kashmir, prompting Maharaja Hari Singh's accession to India. Indian troops halted the invasion, but a third of the state remained under Pakistani occupation (Pakistan-Occupied Kashmir / POK). The UN brokered a ceasefire on *1 January 1949*, creating the *Ceasefire Line (CFL)*.
- *The Indus Waters Treaty (1960):* In *September 1960*, Prime Minister Jawaharlal Nehru and Pakistani President Ayub Khan signed the landmark *Indus Waters Treaty* in Karachi, brokered by the *World Bank*. It divided the six rivers of the Indus basin: the three Eastern rivers (Ravi, Beas, Sutlej) allocated to India, and the three Western rivers (Indus, Jhelum, Chenab) allocated to Pakistan. Despite subsequent wars, the treaty has survived uninterrupted.

== The 1965 War: Shastri's Defiant Leadership & The Tashkent Agreement
- *Pakistani Miscalculation:* Pakistani military dictator Field Marshal Ayub Khan believed that India was politically weak following Nehru's death in 1964 and militarily vulnerable after the 1962 China defeat.
- *The Invasions:* In April 1965, Pakistan launched armed incursions in the *Rann of Kutch* (Gujarat). In August 1965, Pakistan launched *Operation Gibraltar* (sending thousands of armed infiltrators into Jammu and Kashmir) followed by *Operation Grand Slam* (a heavy armor offensive aimed at cutting off the vital Akhnoor bridge).
- *Shastri's Decisive Counter-Attack:* Prime Minister *Lal Bahadur Shastri* coined the immortal rallying slogan *"Jai Jawan, Jai Kisan"* and ordered the Indian Army to launch a massive counter-offensive across the international border in Punjab, advancing toward the outskirts of Lahore and Sialkot.
- *The Tashkent Declaration (10 January 1966):*
  - The Soviet Union mediated peace talks in Tashkent (Uzbekistan), chaired by Soviet Premier *Alexei Kosygin*.
  - Prime Minister Lal Bahadur Shastri and President Ayub Khan signed the *Tashkent Declaration*, agreeing to withdraw armed forces to pre-war positions (5 August 1965 lines) and restore diplomatic ties.
  - *Tragic Denouement:* Hours after signing the agreement on *11 January 1966*, Prime Minister Lal Bahadur Shastri died suddenly of a heart attack in Tashkent, plunging India into national mourning.

== The 1971 Bangladesh Liberation War & The Shimla Agreement
The 1971 war was triggered by the internal democratic explosion in East Pakistan:
- *The Crisis in East Pakistan:* In Pakistan's December 1970 general elections, the Bengali nationalist *Awami League* led by *Sheikh Mujibur Rahman* won an absolute majority. Refusing to transfer power, the West Pakistani military regime under General Yahya Khan launched *Operation Searchlight* on 25 March 1971, slaughtering hundreds of thousands of Bengali intellectuals, students, and citizens.
- *Refugee Influx:* Over *10 million refugees* fled the genocide, crossing into West Bengal, Assam, and Tripura, imposing an intolerable socio-economic strain on India.
- *The Indo-Soviet Treaty (9 August 1971):*
  - Faced with an emerging hostile geopolitical axis between the United States (under Nixon and Kissinger), China, and Pakistan, Prime Minister Indira Gandhi signed a landmark 20-year *Treaty of Peace, Friendship and Cooperation* with the Soviet Union.
  - Article IX stipulated that in the event of an attack on either party, they would immediately consult to remove such threat, providing India with a decisive military shield against US or Chinese military intervention.
- *The 13-Day War (3–16 December 1971):*
  - On 3 December 1971, Pakistan launched pre-emptive airstrikes on Indian airfields. India responded with a coordinated three-front blitzkrieg by the Army, Navy, and Air Force, supporting the Bengali *Mukti Bahini*.
  - The Indian Navy successfully blockaded Karachi port (Operation Trident) and East Pakistani ports (using aircraft carrier INS Vikrant).
  - On *16 December 1971*, Pakistani forces in Dhaka unconditionally surrendered. *Lieutenant General A.A.K. Niazi* signed the historic Instrument of Surrender before Indian Eastern Army Commander *Lieutenant General J.S. Aurora*, surrendering *93,000 Pakistani soldiers*—the largest military surrender since World War II—giving birth to sovereign *Bangladesh*.
- *The Shimla Agreement (2 July 1972):*
  - Prime Minister *Indira Gandhi* and Pakistani President *Zulfikar Ali Bhutto* signed the historic *Shimla Agreement* in Himachal Pradesh.
  - *Core Principles:* Resolved that the two countries would resolve all outstanding differences (including Jammu and Kashmir) strictly through *peaceful, bilateral negotiations*, explicitly excluding third-party international mediation.
  - The 1949 Ceasefire Line was converted into the inviolable *Line of Control (LoC)*.

== The 1999 Kargil Conflict
- In early 1999, Pakistani military forces under Army Chief General Pervez Musharraf secretly crossed the LoC, infiltrating and occupying fortified high-altitude bunker posts in the Kargil, Dras, and Batalik sectors of Ladakh.
- *Operation Vijay:* Indian Armed Forces mounted a ferocious counter-offensive under Prime Minister Atal Bihari Vajpayee, recapturing legendary fortified peaks (Tiger Hill, Tololing) while exercising extraordinary strategic restraint by refusing to cross the international border or the LoC. Pakistan faced diplomatic isolation and withdrew its forces in July 1999.

= 4. India's Nuclear Trajectory: From Idealism to Weaponized Deterrence

India's nuclear policy has evolved as a sophisticated synthesis of moral disarmament advocacy and hard-headed national security realism:

== Origins under Homi J. Bhabha & The Early Policy
- In the late 1940s, nuclear physicist *Dr. Homi J. Bhabha* convinced Jawaharlal Nehru to initiate India's atomic energy program, establishing the Atomic Energy Commission in August 1948.
- Nehru championed the peaceful utilization of nuclear technology for electricity generation, industrial medicine, and agricultural research, while passionately advocating global universal nuclear disarmament.
- *The Security Watershed (1964):* In October 1964, Communist China conducted its first nuclear test at Lop Nur, confronting India with a hostile nuclear-armed neighbor on its northern border.

== Rejecting Discriminatory Treaties: NPT (1968) & CTBT (1996)
- *The Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty (NPT, 1968):* Imposed an arbitrary cutoff date of 1 January 1967, legitimizing the nuclear arsenals of the P5 while banning all other nations from acquiring nuclear capability. India denounced the NPT as "nuclear apartheid" and refused to sign.
- *The Comprehensive Nuclear-Test-Ban Treaty (CTBT, 1996):* India refused to endorse the CTBT, pointing out that it banned physical test explosions while permitting advanced nuclear weapon states to refine warheads through computer simulations, failing to mandate a time-bound schedule for complete global nuclear abolition.

== Pokhran-I (1974) vs Pokhran-II (1998)
- *Pokhran-I (May 1974):* Under Prime Minister Indira Gandhi, Indian scientists conducted India's first underground nuclear detonation at the Pokhran desert range in Rajasthan (code-named *Operation Smiling Buddha*). India characterized the test as a "Peaceful Nuclear Explosion" (PNE), facing punitive Western technology embargoes and the creation of the Nuclear Suppliers Group (NSG).
- *Pokhran-II (May 1998):* On *11 and 13 May 1998*, under Prime Minister *Atal Bihari Vajpayee*, India conducted five underground nuclear detonations at Pokhran (*Operation Shakti*), including a thermonuclear hydrogen bomb test, formally declaring itself an overt nuclear-weapon state.

== India's Nuclear Doctrine
Following the 1998 tests, India codified an official nuclear doctrine predicated upon two cardinal pillars:
- *1. Credible Minimum Deterrence:* Maintaining a survivable, technologically robust nuclear arsenal sufficient only to inflict unacceptable retaliatory damage on an aggressor.
- *2. No First Use (NFU):* Solemnly pledging never to be the first to launch a nuclear strike, committing to nuclear retaliation *only* if Indian territory or forces are attacked with nuclear, biological, or chemical weapons.


= 5. The Indo-US Civil Nuclear Agreement (123 Agreement, 2008)

Following the 1998 nuclear tests and subsequent US sanctions, bilateral diplomacy achieved a historic paradigm shift under Prime Minister Dr. Manmohan Singh and US President George W. Bush:
- *The Strategic Breakthrough (July 2005 – October 2008):* Known as the *Indo-US Civil Nuclear Agreement* (or *123 Agreement* under the US Atomic Energy Act), the pact ended 34 years of international nuclear isolation imposed on India following the 1974 Pokhran-I test.
- *Core Provisions:*
  - India agreed to separate its civilian and military nuclear facilities, placing 14 civilian thermal reactors under permanent *International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA) safeguards*.
  - In exchange, the United States recognized India as a responsible state with advanced nuclear technology, agreeing to full civil nuclear commerce, fuel supplies, and reactor technology transfers.
  - *The NSG Clean Waiver (September 2008):* The 48-nation *Nuclear Suppliers Group (NSG)* granted India a historic, unprecedented "clean waiver", permitting international nuclear commerce with a non-NPT signatory state.
- *Domestic Political Firestorm:* The agreement provoked fierce political contestation in India. The Left Front (CPI and CPI-M) withdrew external parliamentary support from the UPA-I government in July 2008, accusing the government of subordinating Indian foreign policy to American imperialism. Prime Minister Manmohan Singh stood firm, surviving a dramatic parliamentary confidence vote on 22 July 2008.

= 6. The Evolution of Neighborhood Diplomacy: Gujral Doctrine to Act East

India's regional diplomacy evolved progressively to anchor economic integration and counter strategic encirclement:

== The Gujral Doctrine (1996)
- Formulated by External Affairs Minister (and later Prime Minister) *Inder Kumar Gujral* under the United Front government.
- *The Core Principle of Non-Reciprocity:* Recognized that as the preeminent economic and demographic power in South Asia, India must not seek reciprocity from smaller neighbors (Bangladesh, Bhutan, Maldives, Nepal, Sri Lanka), but give what it can in good faith and generosity.
- *Five Principles of the Doctrine:*
  - 1. With smaller neighbors, India does not seek reciprocity, but extends unilateral goodwill and concessions.
  - 2. No South Asian country should allow its territory to be used against another country's security.
  - 3. Non-interference in the internal affairs of neighboring states.
  - 4. Respect for the territorial integrity and national sovereignty of all South Asian nations.
  - 5. Settlement of all bilateral disputes through peaceful, direct bilateral negotiations.

== From "Look East" (1991) to "Act East" (2014)
- *Look East Policy (1991):* Initiated by Prime Minister P.V. Narasimha Rao following the collapse of the Soviet Union to re-integrate India with the dynamic economies of Southeast Asia (ASEAN).
- *Act East Policy (2014):* Upgraded by Prime Minister Narendra Modi, shifting focus from purely commercial engagement to proactive economic connectivity, defense partnerships, maritime security, and cultural diplomacy across the wider Indo-Pacific region.

== Confidence-Building Measures on the Line of Actual Control (LAC)
- *The 1993 and 1996 Agreements:* In September 1993, India and China signed the landmark *Agreement on the Maintenance of Peace and Tranquility along the Line of Actual Control*. This was reinforced in 1996 by an agreement on military Confidence-Building Measures (CBMs), prohibiting the use of firearms, artillery, and combat aircraft within two kilometers of the LAC.
- *The Galwan Valley Flashpoint (June 2020):* Decades of delicate border tranquility collapsed on 15 June 2020 when Chinese troops attempted to alter the status quo in eastern Ladakh, resulting in violent physical clashes in the Galwan Valley that caused the loss of 20 brave Indian soldiers and unconfirmed Chinese casualties—the first fatal military combat on the Sino-Indian border in 45 years.
